/*!
\page page_alamdmc_modules AlamDMC Modules

[TOC]

Each AlamDMC module has its own module-specific configuration, which
is located in the trailing part of the configuration file.
Furthermore, each module will generate its own specific output data.

\section module_asr Multiaxial Stress Test Module (keyword: ASR)

The Multiaxial Stress Test Module prescribes a generic stress loading on a virtual sample.
Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed;
each step imposes a certain (constant) stress mode for a certain deformation (which may be infinitesimally small).

A specific _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The imposed stress state is described with reference to this sample reference frame.

\subsection configuration_asr Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{sample_orientation,euler} specifies the orientation of the sample reference frame of the virtual specimen, in format specified in \ref euler_option.
-# \confdef{n_steps,integer} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps, each one being specified by the configuration settings 3.-4. and their optional subsettings.
These settings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample.
-# \confdef{stress_mode,symsrtensor} specifies the imposed stress mode for given step, with reference to the sample (`xyz`) reference frame, and in format specified in \ref symsrtensor_option.
Note that the hydrostatic part of the imposed stress mode is ignored, as well as its norm.
-# \confdef{update_state,logical} is a switch that controls update of material state for given step. When set to
  - `False` <BR> then \conffield{stress_mode} is merely queried without finite deformation and without associated evolution of state.
  - `True` <BR> then a finite deformation has to be specified as follows:
    -# \conffield{incrementation_type} specifies how the deformation increments will be calculated. The only allowed values in this module are:
		`StrainTensor`, `StrainTensorIncrement` and `PlasticWork`. See \ref incrementation_option for details.
	-# \conffield{step_magnitude} provides maximal deformation (as specified by \conffield{incrementation_type}) to be reached in the virtual test.
	-# \conffield{increment_magnitude} sets the magnitude of deformation increments.

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_asr.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_asr.cfg

\subsection output_asr Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.asr` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{step} The deformation step sequence number
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{increment, increment_ASR} The increment sequence number of the current
	deformation step
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_vM, eps_vM_ASR} The von Mises equivalent strain of the current step
	\f$ \epsilon_{vM,step} \f$

	It is derived from the strain tensor of current step \f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \f$ as:
	\f[
		\epsilon_{vM,step}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{Pnorm} The norm of accumulated absolute plastic strain increment tensors:
		\f[
			P_{norm} = \left\| \sum_{i=1}^{increment} |\Delta\boldsymbol{\epsilon}| \right\|
		\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_total_vM} The von Mises equivalent of the _total_ strain,
	\f$ \epsilon_{vM} \f$

	It is derived from the incremental strain tensor \f$ \Delta \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i} \f$ of an increment \f$ i \f$, as:
	\f[
		\epsilon_{vM}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \sum_{i=1}^{N increments} \Delta \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{W} The accumulated plastic work \f$ W \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dotW} The rate of plastic work \f$ \dot{W} \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{M-factor} The volume-average Taylor factor \f$ \overline{M} \f$

	It is obtained from volume-averaging the sum of microscopic slip rates
	over all deformation systems \f$  \dot{\gamma} \f$, as
	\f[
		\overline{M}= \frac{\overline{ \dot{\gamma} }}{D_{vM}}
	\f]
	The von Mises equivalent strain rate \f$ D_{vM}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \mathbf{D} \| \f$ is
	used as normalization factor.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{scal_s} The norm of a normalized deviatoric stress
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{S, norm_SonA} The norm of deviatoric stress that corresponds to plastic strain mode:
	\f$ \| \mathbf{S}(\mathbf{A}) \| \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{residual} The residual of \ref config_search_procedure "search procedure"
	\f$ Res \f$

	\note As a rule of thumb, the search procedure is supposed to reach the convergence
	if \f$ Res \ll 10^{-2} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{S_11} A component of the deviatoric stress tensor \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$
	in the material (`123`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_22} Similar to \ref S_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_33} Similar to \ref S_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_12} Similar to \ref S_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_23} Similar to \ref S_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_13} Similar to \ref S_11
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{S_xx} A component of the deviatoric stress tensor \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$
	in the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_yy} Similar to \ref S_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_zz} Similar to \ref S_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_xy} Similar to \ref S_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_yz} Similar to \ref S_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{S_xz} Similar to \ref S_xx
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{A_11,asr_A_11} A component of the strain mode tensor \f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ in
	the material (`123`) reference frame

	It is derived from the strain rate tensor \f$ \mathbf{D} \f$ as
	\f[
		\mathbf{A}=\frac{\mathbf{D}}{\| \mathbf{D} \|}
	\f]
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_22} Similar to \ref asr_A_11 "A_11"
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_33} Similar to \ref asr_A_11 "A_11"
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_12} Similar to \ref asr_A_11 "A_11"
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_23} Similar to \ref asr_A_11 "A_11"
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_13} Similar to \ref asr_A_11 "A_11"
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{A_xx} A component of the strain mode tensor \f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ in
	the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_yy} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_zz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_xy} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_yz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_xz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_11} A component of the strain tensor of current step
	\f$ \Delta \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \f$ in the material (`123`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_22} Similar to \ref eps_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_33} Similar to \ref eps_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_12} Similar to \ref eps_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_23} Similar to \ref eps_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_13} Similar to \ref eps_11
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_xx} A component of the strain tensor of current step
	\f$ \Delta \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \f$ in the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_yy} Similar to \ref eps_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_zz} Similar to \ref eps_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_xy} Similar to \ref eps_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_yz} Similar to \ref eps_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_xz} Similar to \ref eps_xx
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_11} A component of the total strain tensor
	\f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \f$ in the material (`123`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_22} Similar to \ref eps_tot_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_33} Similar to \ref eps_tot_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_12} Similar to \ref eps_tot_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_23} Similar to \ref eps_tot_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_13} Similar to \ref eps_tot_11
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_xx} A component of the total strain tensor
	\f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \f$ in the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_yy} Similar to \ref eps_tot_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_zz} Similar to \ref eps_tot_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_xy} Similar to \ref eps_tot_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_yz} Similar to \ref eps_tot_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{eps_tot_xz} Similar to \ref eps_tot_xx
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_qrs Uniaxial Anisotropy Module (keyword: QRS)

The Uniaxial Anisotropy Module provides Lankford coefficients (in two variants: q-values and r-values; cf. below) and normalized uniaxial yield stresses (s-values),
for a specified range of in-plane directions, and under tensile loading. These properties are queried for the virtual microstructure in the given state without
any finite deformation imposed. There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

For each loading case, a _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The loading direction is aligned with the sample `x`-axis, by convention.
The `x`- and `y`-axes are restricted to the material `1`-`2`-plane.
Consequently, the sample `z`-axis is aligned with the material `3`-direction.

\subsection configuration_qrs Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution, real} defines the angle in degrees between consecutive sampling directions in the xy-plane.
-# \conffield{pure_uniaxial_state} is defined [here](@ref pure_uniaxial_state_anchor).
-# \confdef{calculate_Mfactor,logical} is a flag to request calculation of average Taylor factor in module-specific output file. If `True`, it may
increase calculation time for certain model assumptions.
-# \confdef{fold_symmetry,logical} enables or disables averaging of output variables over symmetrically equivalent positions.

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_qrs.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_qrs.cfg

\subsection output_qrs Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.xqrs` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{angle} The in-plane orientation angle, as requested in \conffield{sample_orientation_range}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{rho} The requested imposed stress ratio \f$ \rho \f$, as requested in \conffield{pure_uniaxial_state}. See also \ref stress_ratio_rho
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{q-value} The instantaneous q-value \f$ q_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		q_i = \frac{-D_{yy}}{D_{xx}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{xx}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and tensile
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous r-value as \f$ q_i = \frac{r_i}{1+r_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{r-value} The instantaneous r-value \f$ r_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		r_i = \frac{D_{yy}}{D_{zz}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{zz}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and thickness
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous q-value as
	\f$ \displaystyle r_i = \frac{q_i}{1-q_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{s-value} A normalised flow stress \f$ s \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_xx} The normal component along the x-axis of the total stress
	\f$\sigma_{xx}\f$

	This component of the _total_ stress  \f$\boldsymbol{\sigma}\f$ is derived from
	the deviatoric stress tensor \f$\mathbf{S}\f$ as:
	\f[
		\sigma_{xx}=S_{xx}+\sigma_h=S_{xx}-S_{zz}
	\f]
	in which the hydrostatic stress \f$ \sigma_h \f$ is derived from the assumption that
	the sample is stress-free along the z-axis: \f$ \sigma_{zz}=S_{zz}+\sigma_h=0 \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outref{M-factor}
	</li>

	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_yld Yield Locus Module (keyword: YLD)

The Yield Locus Module calculates generic 2-dimensional sections of the yield locus.
There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

\subsection configuration_yld Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution,real} Angle between sample points, expressed in degrees and measured from the 1st section base vector towards the 2nd section base vector.
-# \confdef{section_default,logical} is a flag to request default or non-default section definition and yield locus scaling.
If \conffield{section_default} equals
  - `True` <BR> then the yield locus section is defined by \f$\sigma_{11}\f$ (normal stress along RD) along the X-axis, and \f$\sigma_{22}\f$ (normal stress along TD) along the Y-axis. Furthermore, the yield locus will be scaled by the yield stress corresponding to uniaxial tension along the RD.
  - `False` <BR> then the following suboptions are required:
    -# \confdef{normalize_base,logical}. If `True`, then the base tensors specified by the following configuration 	settings 2.-3.-4. are normalized so their norm is equal to 1.0.
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_X,symsrtensor} is the base tensor along the X-axis of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_Y,symsrtensor} is the base tensor along the Y-axis of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_offset,symsrtensor} is the base tensor that determines the mode of the stress that coincides with the origin of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{normalize_stress,logical}
	-# \confdef{use_scaling,logical}. If it equals
	  - `False` <BR> then the suboption
        -# \confdef{scaling_stress_mode,symsrtensor} is the stress mode for which the yield point is used as scaling factor, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_yld.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_yld.cfg

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `sid1687f_yld.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno sid1687f_yld.cfg




\subsection output_yld Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.xyld` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{theta,yldout_theta} The angle \f$ \theta \f$ corresponding to a yield locus
	point, as requested in \conffield{theta_range}, expressed in degrees.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{w} The multiplier for the yield locus offset tensor, as requested in
	\conffield{offset_range}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma} The radial coordinate of the yield locus point
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_scaled} The radial coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	</li>

	<li> \outref{S, norm_SonA}
	</li>

	<li> \outref{dotW}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_x} The 1st Cartesian coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	\f$ \sigma_X \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_y} The 2nd Cartesian coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	\f$ \sigma_Y \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dsigma_x} The 1st Cartesian coordinate of the local yield locus normal
	\f$ A_X \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dsigma_y} The 2nd Cartesian coordinate of the local yield locus normal
	\f$ A_Y \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{beta} The angle \f$ \beta \f$ from X-axis to the projection of tensor
	\f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ into section, expressed in degrees.
	It is given as \f$ \beta= \arctan(\frac{A_Y}{A_X}) \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_ewc Equiwork Contour Module (keyword: EWC)

The Equiwork Contour Module produces generic sections of equi-work contours in stress space.
Equi-work contour points are calculated from a series of monotonic stress loading cases within the requested 2-dimensional (\f$X\f$-\f$Y\f$-) section of stress space.
The equi-work contour of plastic work \f$ W=0 \f$ is identical to the yield locus in the initial state of material.

\subsection configuration_ewc Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{worklevel_selection_method,string}
	if \conffield{worklevel_selection_method} is equal to
    - `direct` <BR> the equi-work contour levels are specified directly as a range of plastic work values with \conffield{max_strain} and \conffield{strain_increment}.
	- `reference` <BR> the equi-work contour levels are selected indirectly, on the basis of a reference test. The values of plastic work
	used as contour levels are those for which the von Mises equivalent strain \f$ \epsilon_{vM} \f$ of the reference test are specified
	by \conffield{max_strain} and \conffield{strain_increment}.
	The reference test is specified by the following suboptions:
	  -# \confdef{stress_mode,symsrtensor,stress_mode_EWC} specifies the imposed stress mode of the reference test, in format specified in \ref symsrtensor_option.
	  -# \conffield{incrementation_type} specifies how the deformation increments of the reference test will be calculated. The only values permitted in this module are: `StrainTensor`, `StrainTensorIncrement` and `PlasticWork`. See \ref incrementation_option for details.
	  -# \conffield{step_magnitude} provides maximal deformation (as specified by \conffield{incrementation_type}) to be simulated in the reference test.
		A proper selection of contour levels implies \conffield{step_magnitude} must be set equal or greater than the largest value	of \conffield{contour_levels_range}.
	  -# \conffield{increment_magnitude} sets the magnitude of deformation increments for the reference test.
-# \confdef{angular_resolution,real} defines the angle between subsequent strain modes used as sample points to generate the work contour at each work level. The resolution is expressed in degrees and is measured from the 1st section base vector towards the 2nd section base vector.
-# \confdef{max_strain,real} defines the maximum strain or work to be achieved by the last contour level.
-# \confdef{strain_increment,real} defines the increments of strain or plastic work between different contour levels. Note that if \conffield{max_strain} can not be divided exactly by \conffield{strain_increment}, the final contour level will exceed \conffield{max_strain} to match the specified \conffield{strain_increment}.
-# \confdef{points_per_contour,integer} sets the number of increments to be performed in-between two subsequent contour levels, for all virtual tests that generate the equi-work contour levels.
-# \confdef{use_default_settings,logical} specifies whether or not the default section in stress space is to be used.
If \conffield{use_default_settings} equals
  - `True` then the equi-work contour section is defined by \f$\sigma_{11}\f$ along the
    \f$X\f$-axis, and \f$\sigma_{22}\f$ along the \f$Y\f$-axis.
  - `False` then the following suboptions are required:
    -# \confdef{base_sigma_X,real,base_sigma_X_EWC} is the base tensor along the
       \f$X\f$-axis of the equi-work contour section,
      in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
    -# \confdef{base_sigma_Y,real,base_sigma_Y_EWC} is the base tensor along
       the \f$Y\f$-axis of the equi-work contour section,
       in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_ewc.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_ewc.cfg

\subsection output_ewc Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>_ewc.ewc` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{theta} The angle \f$ \theta \f$ corresponding to a equi-work contour
	point, as requested in \conffield{theta_range}, expressed in degrees
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{S,yld_S} The radial coordinate of the equi-work contour point, for requested
	values of:
	- plastic work \f$ W \f$ (if \conffield{worklevel_selection_method}=`direct`), or
	- von Mises equivalent strain \f$ \epsilon_{vM} \f$ of the reference test
	  (if \conffield{worklevel_selection_method}=`reference`)

	This column is repeated for each of requested contours.
	</li>
</ol>

If \conffield{worklevel_selection_method}=`reference`, two additional output files are
generated: `<output_prefix>_reference.ewc` and `<output_prefix>_contours.ewc`.
Whereas the rows in `<output_prefix>_reference.ewc` correspond to the increments of the
reference test, the rows in `<output_prefix>_contours.ewc` correspond to the contour
levels as requested in \conffield{increment_magnitude}.
Both these outputfiles make column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{eps_vM} The von Mises equivalent strain \f$ \epsilon_{vM} \f$

	It is derived from the strain tensor \f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \f$ as:
	\f[
		\epsilon_{vM}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outref{W}
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_adp Arbirtary Deformation Path (keyword: ADP)

The Arbitrary Deformation Path prescribes an arbitrary generic deformation on a virtual
sample. Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed; each step imposes a certain
(constant) deformation. The module can also be used to simply calculate stress response of
the virtual sample to a superimposed deformation. All inputs and outputs are reported in the
global material frame.

\note This module implements a deformation-driven crystal plasticity model. The only
available option in \ref config_search_procedure is to use the default settings.

\subsection configuration_adp Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{n_steps,integer,n_steps_ADP} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps,
   each one being specified by later configuration settings and their optional
   subsettings. These subsettings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration
   file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample.
-# \confdef{deformation_type,string} specifies how the deformation is imposed in the given
   step. Possible values are:
    - `deformation`

      If this option is used, the next line is:
      -# \confdef{velocity_gradient_tensor,srtensor_voigt} velocity gradient tensor
         \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$ expressed in [Voigt notation](@ref srtensor_option_voigt)

    - `strainmode`

      If this option is used, the next two lines are:
      -# \confdef{strain_mode,symsrtensor} Strain rate mode to be imposed, given in
         [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option). If \conffield{strain_mode} is not of
         unit length, it will be normalized automatically.
      -# \confdef{strain_magnitude,real} Magnitude of strain to be imposed.

    - `strain`

      If this option is used, the next line is:
      -# \confdef{strain,symsrtensor} Strain increment to be imposed during the step,
         given in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option)

-# \confdef{update_state,logical,update_state_ADP} controls updating the material state
   in the given step. When set to:
   - `False` then the stresses and other outputs are queried without finite deformation and
     without associated evolution of state.
   - `True` then the deformation is actually imposed, resulting in evolution of state.

---

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `sid1687f_adp.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno sid1687f_adp.cfg

\subsection output_adp Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.adp` makes column-wise reporting of:
-# \outdef{step,step_ADP} The deformation step sequence number


-# \outdef{increment,increment_ADP} The increment sequence number of the current
   deformation step


-# \outdef{L_11} The 11 component of the velocity gradient tensor \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$ imposed
   during the increment, given in the material (123) reference frame.
-# \outdef{L_22} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_33} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_12} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_23} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_31} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_21} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_32} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_13} similar to \ref L_11

-# \outdef{D_11,adp_D_11} The 11 component of the plastic strain rate, given in the material
   (123) reference frame. \f$ \mathbf{D} \f$ corresponds to the symmetric part of \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$.
-# \outdef{D_22} similar to \ref adp_D_11 "D_11"
-# \outdef{D_33} similar to \ref adp_D_11 "D_11"
-# \outdef{D_12} similar to \ref adp_D_11 "D_11"
-# \outdef{D_23} similar to \ref adp_D_11 "D_11"
-# \outdef{D_13} similar to \ref adp_D_11 "D_11"

-# \outdef{O_12,adp_O_12} The 12 component of the spin rate, given in the material
   (123) reference frame. \f$ \mathbf{O} \f$ corresponds to the
   antisymmetric part of \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$.
-# \outdef{O_23} similar to \ref adp_O_12 "O_12"
-# \outdef{O_13} similar to \ref adp_O_12 "O_12"

-# \outdef{A_11,adp_A_11} The 11 component of the strain mode tensor \f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ in
   the material (123) reference frame. See \ref asr_A_11 "A".
-# \outdef{A_22} Similar to \ref adp_A_11 "A_11"
-# \outdef{A_33} Similar to \ref adp_A_11 "A_11"
-# \outdef{A_12} Similar to \ref adp_A_11 "A_11"
-# \outdef{A_23} Similar to \ref adp_A_11 "A_11"
-# \outdef{A_13} Similar to \ref adp_A_11 "A_11"

-# \outdef{S_11,adp_S_11} The 11 component of the deviatoric stress, given in the material
   (123) reference frame. The deviatoric stress \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$ corresponds to the
   imposed \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$.
-# \outdef{S_22,adp_S_22} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_33,adp_S_33} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_12,adp_S_12} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_23,adp_S_23} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_13,adp_S_13} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"

-# \outdef{eps_vM_begin} Von Mises strain at the beginning of the increment

-# \outdef{eps_vM_end} Von Mises strain at the end of the increment

-# \outdef{D_vM} Von Mises equivalent strain rate

-# \outdef{S_vM} Von Mises equivalent stress

-# \outdef{dW,adp_dotW} plastic work rate \f$ W = \mathbf{D} : \mathbf{S} \f$

-# \outref{M-factor}

-# \outdef{gamma} total accumulated plastic slip


